("P", "Figure 3 shows acceptance of V1 descriptions by association, result realization, and age group. In full-result events, V1 descriptions were accepted almost always (94%–100%). In zero-result events, descriptions with stronger-association V1s were accepted less often than descriptions with weaker-association V1s in every group (4-year-olds: 31% vs. 78%; 5-year-olds: 41% vs. 75%; adults: 68% vs. 98%)."),
("FIG", "fig3"),
("P", "In the model, the association effect in zero-result events was positive in all three groups, with intervals excluding zero for 4-year-olds and adults and only marginally including it for 5-year-olds (difference in log-odds, weaker minus stronger: 4-year-olds: 3.94 [0.46, 7.95], *p*(> 0) = .99; 5-year-olds: 2.87 [−0.49, 6.48], *p*(> 0) = .96; adults: 4.55 [0.69, 9.21], *p*(> 0) = .99; averaged over groups: 3.80 [1.12, 6.84], *p*(> 0) > .99). In full-result events, the association effect was negligible (differences in predicted acceptance ≤ 0.01). The Action–Result Association × Result Realization interaction was positive in all groups but uncertain because acceptance was at ceiling in full-result events (log-odds: 1.49 [−5.39, 8.04], −0.13 [−6.75, 5.75], and 4.46 [−3.54, 12.64])."),
("P", "The association effect did not differ credibly between children and adults. On the log-odds scale, the effect was, if anything, smaller in children (difference: −1.13 [−5.57, 2.91], *p*(> 0) = .29); on the probability scale, it was larger in children (0.31 [−0.30, 0.73], *p*(> 0) = .86), because adults' acceptance of weaker-association V1s was close to ceiling. Both intervals include zero and sizable effects in either direction, so the comparison is inconclusive. The effect also did not differ between 4- and 5-year-olds (−1.05 [−5.23, 2.80]). Children accepted V1 descriptions of zero-result events less often than adults for both stronger-association V1s (difference in predicted acceptance: 0.47 [0.02, 0.82], *p*(> 0) = .98) and weaker-association V1s (0.12 [0.01, 0.55], *p*(> 0) = .99). Children and adults were thus both sensitive to action–result association, and children's stricter judgments held regardless of association."),
("H3", "Sensitivity Analyses and Item-Level Acceptance"),
