("P", "Figure 4 shows acceptance of V1 descriptions in zero-result events for each pair. In seven of the eight pairs, the stronger-association V1 was accepted less often than its weaker-association partner by children, and in six of the eight pairs by adults; the remaining adult pairs were at ceiling for both V1s (Pair 2) or for both groups (Pair 9). The association effect was thus carried by most pairs rather than by one or two. Pair 9 (*cā* \"wipe\" vs. *chōng* \"rinse\" a mirror) behaved differently from the other pairs: Both V1s were accepted in all zero-result trials, and children also accepted the RVC descriptions of the zero-result videos in 50%–75% of trials. One child explained a rejection by saying that only one side of the mirror had been wiped, which suggests that the zero-result videos of this pair showed partial rather than no cleaning. ⟦请核对 P9 的 zero-result 视频⟧ Acceptance of the RVC descriptions in zero-result events was otherwise low for every pair (children: 0%–38%; adults: 0%)."),
